Date:         Wed, 07 Apr 93 10:11:08 CDT
From: "Christopher L. Weeks" <C576653@MIZZOU1.missouri.edu>
Subject: Species Monopoly

Dale Worley, in talking about species monopolies, refers to the situation with
the northern spotted owl which lives in valuable lumber forest.  While not the
way that a business normally considers investment management, there are payoffs
for many individuals which are not in dollars for preserving a species at the
expense of capital growth.  In this case, the investor may capture the benefit
of the owl's existance by "knowing" that (s)he has done a "good" thing in
keeping an owl species alive and "happy".  (S)he may also enjoy the praise of
like-minded individuals which could directly bring cash to the investor.

If a logging company goes to the expennse of retooling their operations so they
create a recyclable plastic wood substitute, they may find a profit based on
public aproval of their actions.  They may also be able to sell the land which
they used to log to private holding companies designed for the purpose of
preserving the wildlife.  I've seen ads for companies which did this on the
local level, and it seems like I've heard of it done nationally--At Waldon
Woods something like this took place.



