From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Tailoring Memes to Win Envirnmentalists

Dan Davis:
> Most people, I believe, think that it is *wrong* to kill animals, cut
> down trees, or destroy a species.

Most people in developed countries may believe this, but most people
on our planet work on farms and do not at all believe this.  The more 
the ecosfascists inhibit the economy, the more people have to stay 
working on the farm, so there is, thankfully, a big negative
feedback here.  Even a recession can have an impact: during the latest
recession environmental group membership declined, and all of a sudden
we had the emotional argument of "jobs & families" to weigh against the
emotional arguments of the ecofascists.   Still, we look forward to
a day when most of our neighbors are extropians without having to
work on a farm!

For those in developed countries, I think it is a case of people
seeing the immediate impact (eg the scenery getting ruined) and
not seeing the indirect economic impact (lumber & housing prices
going up). This is exacerbated by TV which communicates direct 
visual stimuli much better than it communicates indirect cause & 
effect and indirect, non-visual information like market signals.

The ecofascist meme is developing quite functional reproductive
organs.  They've made it a high priority to target the public
schools, to get their message to (usually other people's) children
at an early age.  It's what I would classify as an evangelical
or ultimately parasitical meme -- as opposed to, eg, the Hutterites
and Mormons whose memes largely spread through intrinsic growth,
ie by having & raising their own children.  

A big part of the vector is the stereotypical film footage showing the 
evils of industrial society -- polluting smokestakes, trash,
etc.  Footage of trash dumped at the science station in Antartica 
played a big role in getting oil & mining operations banned from the 
continent for 50 years, for example.  The same thing could happen with 
space development -- trash on the surface of the moon or Mars doesn't get 
biodegraded and looks pretty ugly.   It's easy to react to the 
immediate stimuli and come up with simple solutions (ban it!).  It's
difficult to examine cause & effect, weigh damages & benefits, and
come up with nonintuitive market solutions.

Some ways we could beat the ecofascists:

* Cut off their memetic vectors into our public schools.  If the
want to brainwash children, let them have their own children to
brainwash (of course they won't, the world is "overpopulated" :-)
* Spread extropian-style vectors to children: Heinlein juvies, Teenage 
Mutant Ninja Turtles, etc.
* Most children are still being born on farms in the 3rd world. 
These people are by upbringing anti-ecofascist, but susceptible
to Marxism.  We need to educate them and make them capitalist
allies.
* Implement market solutions (pollution rights & futures, genetic
rights, CO2 & ozone depletion futures, etc.) even if they involve some 
distasteful elements of statism or monopoly.  This is similar to the 
school choice/vouchers movement: it's not completely what we want, but 
it's a big step in the right direction.  If we can replace the bulk of 
the EPA with markets, the cost of "saving the earth" could come way down.
Also, market consensus about the long-range levels of CO2, ozone,
biodiversity, etc. takes the wind out of hysterical predictions and makes 
any real or perceived problems in these areas easier for the market
to deal with.

