is the work of Saed Younis and Tom Knight, who missed the workshop.
They have a draft paper in preparation with the title "Practical
Implementation of Charge Recovering Asymptotically Zero Power
CMOS".  For more information, contact younis@ai.mit.edu).

I have a paper that will appear "soon" in Nanotechnology titled
"Reversible Electronic Logic Using Switches."  This paper has
been in preparation since early 1991.

Quick summary:

Energy dissipation during switching is generally thought to be
1/2 CV^2.  The common realization in most of the new papers is
that the switching losses can be made smaller, and are described
by the equation:

CV^2 * RC/T

Where
C is the capacitance being charged
     (the gate capacitance, for example).
R is the resistance through which the charge must flow
     (the "on" resistance of the FET, the wiring, and such like).
V is the voltage (typically 5 volts, though getting lower recently).
T is the time during which the charging process takes place.

By slowing down the speed of computation (increasing T) we can reduce
the energy dissipated during the computation as much as desired.
(Note that this equation should not be viewed as fundamental.  It
applies to a broad range of computational devices that charge and
discharge condensers by moving charge through conventionally resistive
wires or switches during the charging process.  There are many models
of computation to which this equation does not apply).

Conventional CMOS circuits can't use this equation but are stuck with
the older 1/2 CV^2.  Properly designed reversible CMOS circuits can
take advantage of this equation.  Even though energy dissipation is
far above kT, the reversible circuits can dissipate less energy than
conventional circuits.  Thus, reversible logic is emerging as a useful
heuristic even when energy dissipation is quite large.

There are various constant factors involved (e.g., the area occupied by the
reversible circuits is greater and the speed is slowed by some constant
amount when a circuit is "translated" into the new form).  These factors
are currently not well understood (this field is very new) and depend very
strongly on the precise details of the implementation.  They are being
investigated.  There are also issues surrounding the design of the power
supply (which is different for the reversible designs).  Real circuits
can and have been built, so these problems can be solved in the real world.
The question at the moment is the competitiveness of these schemes against
conventional approaches.  I would expect that in niche markets where
power is limited, budgets aren't and speed of computation is not the
primary objective that the reversible approaches will prove competitive.
Given that the trend in computation is towards lower energy dissipation
per logic operation, I would expect that these niche applications would
gradually expand into the computing mainstream over the next few decades.

In the long run, I expect thermodynamically reversible computing to
dominate computation (though possibly not the form of reversible
computing described by the above equation).

