Subject: Korzybski's "E-Prime", also NLP

I wonder how much several modern schools of thought borrow from GS
(General Semantics), or whether there is a common ancestor. 

For example, cognitive science seems in many ways similar to GS.
Cognitive therapy is the first form of "talk therapy" that has
been demonstrated to have a significant effect treating depression.
It's as effective as psychoactive drugs like Prozac; overall a 
combination of drug & cognitive therapy is often most effecitve.

The goal of cognitive therapy is to remove "cognitive distortions",
or irrational beliefs a person has about himself that is causing
a spiral of negative thoughts that leads to depression.  In GS
terms, the patient has a very innacurate map of himself or other
people, but thinks as if it was was accurate.   For example:

* All-or-nothing thinking: you see things in black-or-white categories.
  If your performance falls short of perfect, you see yourself as
  a total failure.
* Overgeneralization: you see a single negative event as a never-ending
  pattern of defeat.
* Mental filter: you pick out a single mental detail and dwell on
  it exclusively.
* Mind reading: you automatically conclude that someone is reacting
  negatively to you, and you don't bother to check it out.
* The fortune teller error: you anticipate that things will turn
  out badly, and you feel convinced that your prediction is an
  already-established fact.
* Emotional reasoning: you assume that your negative emotions 
  necessarily reflect the way things are: "I feel it, therefore
  it must be true".
* etc.

In cognitive therapy the counselor & patient discuss specific
things that have happened to the patient & how the patient thinks
and feels about them.  The counselor points out where the patient
is making these errors.  Many patients can train themselves to
recognize and avoid these distortions, and thereby get rid of their
depression and lead more effective lives. 

Much of that sounds straight out of Korzybski, but specifically targetted
towards depression.  The only thing missing is that the cognitive
scientists don't credit Korzybski, thus I wonder whether the two
have a common ancestor or if Korzybski is being unjustly snubbed.
In any case, I think the NLP folks should dump the last vestiges
Freud and start talking to the cognitive folks.

I'm also quite curious about some interesting similarities between 
a couple Korzybski inventions and modern software engineering
techniques.  Korzybski promoted a non-Aristotolean logic: many-valued
instead of two-valued, based on "maximum probability" instead of
certainty.  Today fuzzy logic, based on the same principles,
is a rapidly growing industry, especially in Japan and China where they 
are not hung up on two-valued logic.  Just as with Korzybski, fuzzy
logic is practically nonexistant at Western universities, despite
its commercial value and staggering intellectual implications.

I also see many similarities between the structural differential and
object-oriented programming.  OO is becoming widely useful but
has, like the structural differential, received little rigorous
theoretical attention.  In both cases, the goal is to create
structures that map the world, abstracting out shared characteristics 
to higher levels of abstraction, but recognizing that lower levels of 
abstraction can model the world better, and being careful not to 
confuse levels of abstraction.  Software engineering might benefit 
greatly by comparing the strategies used for constructing class 
heirarchies with Korzybski's recommendations.

